## Architecture

This app is built on the [platform-infra](https://github.com/{{ github_handle }}/platform-infra) stack.

| Layer | Technology |
|---|---|
| Backend | FastAPI (Python 3.13), served via DigitalOcean App Platform |
| Database | PostgreSQL 18 on a shared managed cluster (private VPC) |
| Cache / Queue | Valkey 8 on a shared managed instance (Redis-compatible; DO replaced Managed Redis with Valkey in mid-2025) |
{% if include_frontend -%}
| Frontend | Next.js (TypeScript), deployed to DigitalOcean App Platform |
{% endif -%}
{% if include_ios -%}
| iOS | SwiftUI |
{% endif -%}
| Infrastructure | Pulumi (Python), stack `{{ pulumi_org }}/{{ app_name }}/prod` |
| Auth | Clerk |
| Feature Flags | Flagsmith |
| Error Tracking | Sentry |
| Observability | Honeycomb |

## Local Development

### Prerequisites

- Python 3.13+, [`uv`](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/)
{% if include_frontend -%}
- Node.js 24+
{% endif -%}
- Docker (for local Postgres + Valkey)

### Backend

```bash
# Copy and fill in environment variables (pydantic-settings reads from backend/.env)
cp backend/.env.example backend/.env

# Start local Postgres + Valkey
docker compose up -d

# Install dependencies and run
cd backend
uv sync
uv run uvicorn app.main:app --reload
```

The API will be available at http://localhost:8000.
{% if include_frontend %}

### Frontend

```bash
cd frontend
cp .env.example .env.local
npm install
npm run dev
```

The frontend will be available at http://localhost:3000.
{% endif %}

## Tests

```bash
cd backend
uv run pytest
```

## Environment Variables

See `backend/.env.example`{% if include_frontend %}, `frontend/.env.example`,{% endif %} and the root
`.env.example` reference for required variables and documentation. In production,
these are injected by Pulumi as App Platform secrets — you never manage them manually.

| Variable | Description |
|---|---|
| `DATABASE_URL` | PostgreSQL connection string (asyncpg driver) |
| `REDIS_URL` | Redis connection URL |
| `DEBUG` | Enable debug mode (default: `false`) |
| `ENVIRONMENT` | Runtime environment (`development` / `production`) |
{% if include_frontend -%}
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` | Backend API URL for the frontend |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` | Clerk publishable key for the frontend |
{% endif -%}
| `CLERK_SECRET_KEY` | Clerk authentication secret |
| `FLAGSMITH_API_KEY` | Flagsmith server-side environment key |
| `SENTRY_DSN` | Sentry DSN for error reporting |
| `HONEYCOMB_API_KEY` | Honeycomb ingest key for observability |

## Deploying to the shared platform

You need: an `app-deployer` DigitalOcean token and Pulumi access (ask your
platform admin to grant you — see platform-infra → "Granting a new app-owner").

1. Make sure the GitHub repo exists, has a pushed `main` branch, and is visible
   to DigitalOcean App Platform. For private repos, grant the DigitalOcean
   GitHub app access to this repository in GitHub. Stop on DigitalOcean's
   create-app page; Pulumi creates the app.
2. Run the generated preflight checklist:
   ```bash
   bash scripts/preflight-new-app.sh
   ```
3. Initialise your app stack, set config, and deploy — all from `infra/`:
   ```bash
   cd infra
   pulumi stack init prod           # first time only — you create and own this stack
   # Runtime SaaS values:
   pulumi config set --secret clerk_secret_key  <value>
   pulumi config set --secret flagsmith_api_key <server-side environment key>
   pulumi config set --secret sentry_dsn        <value>
   pulumi config set --secret honeycomb_api_key <ingest key>
{%- if include_frontend %}
   pulumi config set clerk_publishable_key pk_test_<value>   # frontend only, non-secret
{%- endif %}
   pulumi up                        # creates your app + per-app database/user/pool
   ```
   The first App Platform deployment may fail because the app is not yet trusted
   by the shared database/Valkey firewalls — that is expected until onboarding.
4. **Ask your platform admin to onboard this app (one-time):**
   `./scripts/onboard-app.sh {{ app_name }}` (run in platform-infra). This makes
   your app a trusted source and grants your DB user schema privileges.
5. Redeploy (push to `main`, or re-run `pulumi up` from `infra/`).
6. In the Flagsmith dashboard, create the `hello_banner` flag (enabled) for your
   environment so `/feature` reports it on.
7. Get your live URL and verify:
   ```bash
   pulumi stack output app_url                  # https://{{ app_name }}-xxxx.ondigitalocean.app
   curl "$(pulumi stack output app_url)/api/"   # all integrations should report true
   bash ../scripts/verify-live.sh
   ```

Once onboarded, merging to `main` triggers GitHub Actions (`pulumi up` + App
Platform redeploy) automatically — no manual steps needed for subsequent deploys.

## Credentials reference

| Credential | Where it lives | Who provides it |
|---|---|---|
| `DIGITALOCEAN_TOKEN` (`app-deployer`) | local env + GitHub Actions secret | you (issued by admin) |
| `PULUMI_ACCESS_TOKEN` | local + GitHub Actions secret | you |
| `clerk_secret_key` | Pulumi config secret | you (Clerk **Development** instance) |
| `clerk_publishable_key` | Pulumi config (non-secret) | you (frontend only) |
| `flagsmith_api_key` | Pulumi config secret | you (server-side environment key) |
| `sentry_dsn` | Pulumi config secret | you |
| `honeycomb_api_key` | Pulumi config secret | you (ingest key) |
| Trusted source + schema grant | platform clusters | **platform admin** (onboard-app.sh) |

## Post-Scaffold Setup

After scaffolding this repo for the first time:

**1. Run the preflight checklist:**

```bash
bash scripts/preflight-new-app.sh
```

**2. Create or reuse per-app SaaS resources:**

```bash
# Optional setup-only tokens. Skip these if you created/reused resources manually.
export FLAGSMITH_SERVER_API_KEY=...
export SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=...
export SENTRY_ORG=...
export HONEYCOMB_CONFIGURATION_API_KEY=...

bash scripts/setup-saas.sh
```

Collect the runtime values for Pulumi config:

- Clerk Development secret key (`sk_test_...`)
{% if include_frontend -%}
- Clerk Development publishable key (`pk_test_...`)
{% endif -%}
- Flagsmith server-side environment key
- Sentry DSN
- Honeycomb ingest key

**3. Initialize Pulumi stack:**

```bash
cd infra
pulumi stack init prod   # first time only — creates the stack in Pulumi Cloud
```

**4. Store the runtime keys as Pulumi config secrets** (this is what injects them into the app at runtime via App Platform):

```bash
pulumi config set --secret clerk_secret_key  "sk_test_..."
pulumi config set --secret flagsmith_api_key "..."   # server-side environment key
pulumi config set --secret sentry_dsn        "https://...@sentry.io/..."
pulumi config set --secret honeycomb_api_key "..."   # ingest key
{%- if include_frontend %}
# Frontend only — the Clerk publishable key is required but NOT secret
# (it is inlined into the static bundle at build time):
pulumi config set clerk_publishable_key "pk_test_..."
{%- endif %}
```

**5. Add CI credentials as GitHub Actions secrets** (for CI/CD deployments):

```bash
gh secret set PULUMI_ACCESS_TOKEN --body "..."
gh secret set DIGITALOCEAN_TOKEN  --body "..."
```

**6. Deploy infrastructure:**

For local deployment, export the same tokens as environment variables:

```bash
export PULUMI_ACCESS_TOKEN="pul-..."
export DIGITALOCEAN_TOKEN="dop_v1_..."

pulumi up
```

(In CI/CD, GitHub Actions automatically uses the secrets from step 5)

## GitHub Actions Secrets Required

| Secret | Description |
|---|---|
| `PULUMI_ACCESS_TOKEN` | Pulumi Cloud token (used by deploy workflow) |
| `DIGITALOCEAN_TOKEN` | DigitalOcean API token (used by deploy workflow) |

SaaS credentials (`CLERK_SECRET_KEY`, `FLAGSMITH_API_KEY`, `SENTRY_DSN`,
`HONEYCOMB_API_KEY`) are stored as **Pulumi config secrets**, not GitHub secrets.
Pulumi injects them directly into App Platform as runtime environment variables.

## Feature Flags

Feature flags are managed in [Flagsmith](https://flagsmith.com). To add a flag:

1. Create the flag in the Flagsmith dashboard for this project
2. Read it in Python:
   ```python
   from flagsmith import Flagsmith
   flagsmith = Flagsmith(environment_key=settings.flagsmith_api_key)
   is_enabled = flagsmith.is_feature_enabled("my-feature")
   ```

